WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.519
From Lupita Nyong'o's bravura double performance
in Us to John Travolta overacting in the worst

00:00:06.519 --> 00:00:11.580
way in The Fanatic, here's a look back at
the highest highs and lowest lows of 2019

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.700
in movie acting.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.660
In Us, Lupita Nyong'o makes the most of a
rare opportunity to put two totally different

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.420
performances in the same film.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.660
While playing Adelaide, Nyong'o is anxious
and twitchy, with her emotions building throughout

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.460
the film as she tries to protect her family.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.740
While playing Adelaide's counterpart Red,
Nyong'o is full of grace and danger, with

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.960
such an unsettling presence that you almost
want to flinch when she appears on screen.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.640
Together, Nyong'o plays the full spectrum
of emotions between two identical characters

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.670
that never once leaves the viewer confused
about who's who.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.540
It's an absolutely unshakeable pair of performances
that carry the film and only become more impressive

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:51.460
on a rewatch.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.220
"The shadow hated the girl.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.160
So much.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.560
For so long."

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.720
Jessica Chastain seemed like a perfect fit
to play the otherworldly alien Vuk as she

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.560
tries to bring Jean Grey over to the dark
side in Dark Phoenix.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.390
Unfortunately, while the third-act climax
and script can be blamed on behind-the-scenes

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.940
drama, that doesn't excuse how boring her
performance in the film is.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.500
As Vuk, Chastain should be strange and seductive,
an alluring contrast to the men in Jean's

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.100
life that are trying to control her.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:29.140
Instead, Chastain just seems bored, delivering
dialogue as if she's reading the script for

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.000
the first time.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.980
"What remains of my people searched the stars
for that power to control it, but it destroyed

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.920
everything it ever came into contact with…until
you."

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.340
In Midsommar, Florence Pugh plays Dani as
reserved, introverted, and shell-shocked following

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.880
her family's sudden death in the opening scene
of the film.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.670
Those feelings only intensify once she gets
a last-minute guilt invite to her boyfriend's

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.640
vacation to a pagan festival that involves
psychedelic drugs and ritualistic killing.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:03.840
A lesser talent might have played Dani's grief
and probable PTSD like a boiling kettle that

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.680
slowly explodes; instead, Pugh shows a more
naturalistic depiction of grief.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.040
She's looking for comfort from her no-good
boyfriend, and trying to stay on top of her

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.060
emotions while taking shrooms.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.740
When her grief finally explodes out of her
in a ritual of shared pain and screaming,

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.080
it takes your breath away.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.560
If John Travolta's starring role in 2018's
mega-flop Gotti wasn't bad enough, he delivered

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.420
another stinker in 2019's The Fanatic.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.660
The film follows an autistic man named Moose
who becomes obsessed with a horror film actor.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.460
That obsession soon curdles into an obsessive
hate, which leads to Moose stalking and threatening

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.800
his favorite star.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.280
Even if you could get past the offensive stereotypes
that Travolta uses as shorthand to communicate

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:53.040
that Moose isn't neurotypical, the actor just
can't handle playing a character that needs

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.780
to be both sympathetic and sociopathic at
different points in the film.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:02.220
Maybe Travolta just needs one good film to
get back on top, but The Fanatic definitely

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.020
isn't it.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.160
Like Blade Runner, another science fiction
film with a powerfully ambiguous lead performance

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.150
and a burdensome voiceover attached to the
theatrical version, Ad Astra feels like a

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.160
film destined to become a cult classic in
the decades to come.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.180
While the film has a dense script and some
impressive visual set pieces, the core of

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.200
Ad Astra depends on Brad Pitt giving one of
the most nuanced performances of his career.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.920
As Roy McBride, Pitt is a son in search of
his father.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Not just in a metaphorical way, either; he's
literally tasked by SpaceCom to head out to

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.250
Neptune to search for his dad in order to
stop a series of power surges that threatens

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.600
the entire solar system.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:56.180
Pitt shines in the role, delivering every
ounce of nuance and backstory with only a

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.840
few clenches of the jaw or far-away look.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:05.300
M. Night Shymalan's early superhero film Unbreakable
ends on an ambiguous note that seemed like

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.840
it would never be resolved.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:13.220
That is, until Bruce Willis' David Dunn made
a cameo at the end of 2017's Split, revealing

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.920
that the two films took place in the same
universe.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:22.960
That sets up 2019's Glass, a film that seemed
to promise the long-awaited sequel to Unbreakable.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.500
The good news for fans is that it came out.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:30.810
The bad news is that the film itself is largely
terrible, squandering the promise of Unbreakable's

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.720
final moments.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.120
Glass has plenty wrong with it, but Willis'
poor performance would rank high on the list.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.440
In Unbreakable, Willis played Dunn with a
quiet melancholy in a reserved, almost deadpan

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:42.940
performance.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.360
It's a fine line to tread as an actor, and
in Glass, Willis misses the mark entirely.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:53.880
What was once an internal, carefully-calibrated
sadness has become a boredom so obvious that

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:57.970
Willis was barely mentioned in reviews of
the film, despite playing one of the most

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:58.960
prominent roles.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:05.400
Over the course of five seasons, Aaron Paul's
eternally hard-luck addict Jesse Pinkman was

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.720
basically the moral center of Breaking Bad.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:12.980
Even in a show beloved for incredible performances,
Paul's acting throughout was a standout.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.260
That's partly why it was so mystifying that
he didn't become a major star after the show's

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:17.760
end.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.200
Besides leading roles in 2014's Need For Speed
and the prestige television show The Path,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:29.000
Paul's largely stuck to cameos, small roles,
or voice acting work.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.840
El Camino, the film sequel/epilogue to Breaking
Bad, shows exactly what Hollywood's been missing

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.220
by reminding everyone how beautifully Paul
can inhabit a role.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:43.140
His performance against acting heavyweights
like Jonathan Banks, Bryan Cranston, and Robert

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.900
Forster make it clear that Paul is one of
the best actors around.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.500
"You don't watch the news?"

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.140
"I have, very much so."

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.640
"So you been hearing what they've been saying
about me."

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.440
Even the best actors can have misfires, and
Matthew McConaughey is no exception.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.980
Case in point: the bizarre misfire Serenity,
which ranks near the bottom of the actor's

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:05.700
career output.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.900
McConaughey is no stranger to turning stereotypical
characters into fully fleshed-out people,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:14.800
but that same instinct seems to have worked
against Serenity.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.780
Considering the laughably silly plot twist,
it might have been better for McConaughey

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:24.130
to play the role with his tongue slightly
in cheek rather than commit on the level of

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.860
his time on True Detective.

